ill looks desperately ill but  doctors say he should survive  many like him haven't  the ward is full they're putting  patience now on the floor  one-year-old mohammed has been given a  nasal tube to feed him urgently needed  fluids  ward dr abdul rahman says he's afraid of  what could be on the way  the rains are not come early the city  will come once day by day and the number  of bridges that will be increasing even  hour by hour day by day and we cannot  handle it or we cannot save them  right across the horn of africa they are  bracing themselves for the double impact  of war and famine  the worst drought in 40 years is  combining with the effects of russia's  invasion of ukraine the ball there may  be thousands of miles away from these  markets but it's pushing up the prices  of grain and fuel to unprecedented  levels making the impact of the drought  even worse  [Music]  the drought now threatens the worst  famine for years camps are already  filling  with people who've lost any means of  supporting their families  the parents are desperate  [Music]  like sofia a few months ago well off by  her standards she's now destitute she's  had to move to the camp after the  drought killed all her livestock  my grandfather was a goat herd my father  was a goat herd  i grew up in that life and ended up with  500 goats and four camels and they've  all been wiped out i've got nothing now  [Music]  the rains have failed for a fourth year  with no grass left for the goats they  pull leaves from trees to eat  but getting food to the people who need  it will be a huge challenge  across the region millions are facing  acute hunger in the worst drought in  over a generation compounded by the war  in ukraine and without massive  international help agencies say hundreds  of thousands will die of starvation  dominic waghorn sky news mandera  somaliland  well let's get a bit more of today's  climate news now firefighters in spain  are struggling to contain wildfires  after an unusually early heat waves saw  temperatures reach over 40 degrees down  the northwestern province of samora is  among the worst hit areas more than 61  000 acres of forest were destroyed in  the area and hundreds of people  evacuated the biodiversity hot spots is  home to one of the largest wolf  populations in western europe  now china has also been experiencing a  heat wave with power consumption surging  in some areas now the warmer  temperatures mean homes and businesses  are demanding more power as they switch  on air conditioning to try to stay cool  now the province of henan home to a  hundred million people set a new record  yesterday using over 65 million  kilowatts that's according to state  media longer hotter summers caused by  climate change could force china to  ration power consumption during these  periods  colombia has elected gustavo petro as  its first left-wing president the former  guerrilla fighter has vowed to end oil  exploration it's controversial in  colombia as it's a major source of  exports earnings francia marquez a  long-time environmental activist will  become the country's first black female  vice president  the lawyer was forced to flee her home  after receiving death threats for  opposing illegal gold mining in her area  now more than 60 people have died in  flooding across india and bangladesh  with millions of homes underwater now  both countries have called in the  military to help rescue those stranded  the flooding in bangladesh has been  described by government officials as  potentially the country's worst since  2004.  now the extreme monsoon rains have hit  large parts of northern bangladesh and  northeastern india and it follows just  weeks after pre-monsoon flash floods  from india's north eastern states and  that had a huge impact on bangladesh  well joining us now is dr saiful islam  he's director of the institute of water  and flood management at bangladesh  university of engineering and technology  thanks so much for joining me dr islam  so first of all we know that bangladesh  is prone to flooding and monsoon rain of  course but how do you think climate  change has impacted the situation  we have uh seen  devastating uh flood uh recent uh  times and particularly this year we have  seen huge amount of rainfall occur in  the episteme catchment particularly  north east bangladesh and  the flood in the past we have seen  bangladesh is known for flood but this  time the amount of water come and it  stays within very short time  and many cities in the northeast  countries not this part of the country  has been underwater for several days and  we feel like the pattern of  flooding is changing  successive floods and every year we find  floods and now other part of the country  as you mentioned the north and north  central also affected so  these are the signals that  weather pattern has changed it becomes  more frequent and intense that has been  mentioned in the last ipcc report sixth  assessment and i was a contributor to  the report as lead author so  things are changing and it's become more  [Music]  vulnerable country become more  vulnerable and we know dr islam that  bangladesh has had huge success in  mitigating climate change but how  prepared do you think flood preparation  is in the country  actually uh as i mentioned that we have  a role model for  managing uh different disaster  particularly flood and cyclone but this  time government is also big challenge  because  everywhere 90 percent of the city is  under water which has not been in the  case in the past so uh even the airport  is under water the power station  the hospitals houses everything is under  water so even rescue relief or operation  on uh particular districts is very  difficult and we are not that much  prepared actually and we have to think  how can manage in the future what needs  to be done as a priority as the uh  recent time and also the long-term plans  okay dr islam thank you very much uh for  talking to u